;;; A rule's `:assert` splits a name that starts with `or` — **W22**, register
;;; row `W22/kw-or-in-assert` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`): a reserved word is
;;; a keyword only as a whole token (Q-M1e2.17).
;;;
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ──────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The witness the family was found by: `kw-neq-in-assert.ein` with the
;;; relation `orz` in place of `neqw`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A keyword is a whole word: `grammar.md` § 1, fact 1, excludes a reserved
;;; word from `SYMBOL` only where a word boundary follows it (`neq` is not a
;;; `SYMBOL`; `neq_test` is), so `orz` is a name like any other. `r` concludes
;;; `(orz a)`: root holds `(orz a)` and `(p a)`, H is empty and R is {∅}. The
;;; query's one class is `?y = a`, the answer of `kw-or-in-assert-twin.ein`.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and why it does not any more ────────────
;;;
;;; At `35186bc0` the parser read a reserved word as a prefix of a name, and
;;; this program answered wrong. The parser read `(orz ?x)` as the keyword `or`
;;; followed by `z`, so `r` stored `(or z a)`, a fact of the reserved `or`. `z`
;;; became a name of the program, H was `(orz a)`, `(orz z)` and `(p z)`, and R
;;; was their eight subsets. The query printed as `(or z ?y)`. (`ein solve FILE`
;;; at `35186bc0`, release and debug; every mode and `ein oracle` the same.) A
;;; keyword is a whole word now, at the boundary `grammar.md` § 1 states
;;; (Q-M1e2.17), and the program answers its twin's R.
;;;

(relation p Node :why "p holds of {?1}") (relation orz Node :why "orz holds of {?1}")
(p a)
(rule r () :match (p ?x) :assert (orz ?x) :why "p holds of {?x}, so orz holds of {?x}")
(query :goal (orz ?y))
